preConditionHook:
// A PULLBACK NO DEEPER THAN THE CHANNEL'S OWN HISTORY. The bar dips below the
// prior bar's low, but by no more than dipTicks (mirrored: pokes above the
// prior bar's high by no more than that). T16-05 and R28-18 are the same limit
// order one to four ticks below the prior bar's low inside a micro channel,
// sized from the channel's largest pullback so far: "if the biggest pullback has
// been five ticks, buy any three- or four-tick pullback". Default 4 from T16-05;
// R28-18 gives no number.
//
// A BAND, so it is NOT DipBelowPriorBarLow at a parameter and not that rule
// negated: the UPPER bound is the whole point. DipBelowPriorBarLow asks for AT
// LEAST dipTicks of dip and gets deeper as its input rises; this one asks for a
// dip of AT MOST dipTicks and gets looser as its input rises, and the two fire
// on disjoint sets of bars whenever both bounds bite. A deep dip is a different
// event, which is why the merge kept them apart.
//
// The lower edge is STRICT (the bar must actually have dipped), the upper edge
// is not, matching the sources' "one to four ticks below". Both terms read bar 1
// unconditionally, so EL's lack of short-circuiting costs nothing here.
//
// Not CloseNearPriorLow, which tests the close against low[1] plus an offset
// with no lower bound.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local. Every scratch value is a localVariables entry,
// never a hook-scope declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
